                <pb facs="0412" n="300" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg300"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 774/709.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador O’Brien to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Tokio</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-02-22"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">February 22, 1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1057.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the embassy’s dispatch of
                    October 13, 1909,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d277-f1"/> in which Mr. Jay acknowledged
                    the receipt of the department’s circular instruction of September 1, 1909,
                    entitled “International Opium Conference,” I have the honor to inclose herewith
                    a copy of a note from the Minister of Foreign Affairs, dated February 21, in
                    which he states that the Japanese Government, while having no objection in
                    principle to the holding of such an international conference, requires still
                    further time to consider some of the points raised in the tentative
                    programme.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. J. O’Brien.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d277-a1.xml" xpointer="d277-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
